-82059/selection/88855ad610d514a28e4405dd951fe296ed27d844d220ac5ef80ea0d8bdc52817" "q-82059/selection/e0b5c330f193506986cafc5a146aca7a001afb9b9ce56ab0831626a2b5837a08" "q-82059/selection/021c22efb7bf4f335fefbe1dc51924d3124acac32d7aa51b177c128b770d8602"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82059" :question/id #uuid "e5756e8a-d13a-5d5f-9a98-ccb200a75413" :question/math-academy-id "q-82059" :question/problem "What is the geometrical interpretation of the transformation represented by the matrix $A$ given below? \n \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nComparing $A$ with the rotation matrix, we have \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ are positive, we must have $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nDividing the first equation by the second gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians anticlockwise about the origin." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-82059/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea036e3-19cd-572b-a6cb-2fa466c2a399"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82059"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-279425/field-1/ccc5b7f3cfdb4c2dfc0e1f31be54559a1ab3ada